Keywords: productive memory distortion, counterfactual simulation, causal invariance, spurious liability, rate–distortion theory, non-homomorphic transformation, abstraction, forgetting, falsifiability.


PART I — MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATIONS

1. Measure-Theoretic and Probabilistic Preliminaries

1.1 Kernels and Total Variation

Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{B}, \mathbb{P})$ be a probability space. For measurable spaces $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}})$ and $(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{Y}})$, a Markov kernel $K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$ is a map $K: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{Y}} \to [0,1]$ with $x \mapsto K(x, B)$ measurable for each $B$ and $B \mapsto K(x, B)$ a probability measure for each $x$. Composition $LK$ is defined by $(LK)(x, C) = \int_{\mathcal{Y}} L(y, C) K(x, dy)$ and is associative.

Definition 1.1 (Total variation). For probability measures $\mu, \nu$ on $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}})$,
\|\mu - \nu\|_{TV} = \sup_{A \in \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}}} |\mu(A) - \nu(A)| = \tfrac{1}{2} \int |d\mu - d\nu|.


For kernels $K, L: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$,
\|K - L\|_{TV} = \sup_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \|K(x, \cdot) - L(x, \cdot)\|_{TV}.

Proposition 1.2 (Cross-entropy continuity under TV). Let $P, Q, Q'$ be probability measures on a finite set $\mathcal{Y}$ with $Q'(y) \ge \alpha > 0$ for all $y$. Then
\left| \mathbb{E}_P[-\log Q] - \mathbb{E}_P[-\log Q'] \right| \le \frac{1}{\alpha}\|Q - Q'\|_1 \le \frac{2}{\alpha}\|Q - Q'\|_{TV}.

Proof. For each $y$, the mean value theorem gives $|\log Q(y) - \log Q'(y)| \le |Q(y) - Q'(y)| / \min_y Q'(y) \le |Q(y) - Q'(y)|/\alpha$. Multiplying by $P(y) \le 1$ and summing yields the first bound; the second follows from $\|\cdot\|_1 \le 2\|\cdot\|_{TV}$. $\square$

Corollary 1.3. If $Q_n \to Q$ in TV and $Q$ is bounded below by $\alpha > 0$, then $\mathrm{CE}(P, Q_n) \to \mathrm{CE}(P, Q)$.

1.2 Structural Causal Models

Definition 1.4 (SCM). A structural causal model is a tuple $W = (U, V, F)$ with exogenous $U$ jointly independent, endogenous $V = (V_1, \dots, V_k)$, structural equations $V_i = f_i(\mathrm{pa}(V_i), U_i)$ with $\mathrm{pa}(V_i) \subseteq V \cup U \setminus \{V_i\}$, and $G(W)$ acyclic. We specialize to
C = f_C(U_C), \quad S = f_S(C, U_S), \quad Y = f_Y(C, S, U_Y),


with $C$ invariant causal factors, $S$ spurious/contextual factors, $Y$ the target. The action is $X = (X_C, X_S)$.

Definition 1.5 (Intervention and counterfactual). For $Z \subseteq \{C,S,Y\}$ and value $z$, $do(Z=z)$ replaces the structural equations for $Z$ with constants. The counterfactual $V_i(z)$ is the value of $V_i$ in $W_{do(Z=z)}$ under the same exogenous $U$.

Definition 1.6 (Counterfactual-invariant $\sigma$-algebra). For $Z \subseteq S$,
\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp} = \sigma\bigl(\{X(z)\}_z\bigr) \cap \sigma(X).

Theorem 1.7 (Veitch et al., 2021). A kernel $P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde{M})$ satisfies
P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde{M}) = P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \mid \widetilde{M}) \quad \forall Z \subseteq S,\, \forall z


if and only if $P_{\mathrm{sim}}$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable.

1.3 Optimal Transport

Let $(\mathcal{E}, d)$ be a compact metric space, hence Polish. For probability measures $\mu, \nu$ on $\mathcal{E}$, let $\Gamma(\mu, \nu)$ be the set of couplings.

Definition 1.8 (Wasserstein). For continuous cost $c: \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E} \to [0, \infty)$,
W_c(\mu, \nu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \mathbb{E}_{(e, \tilde{e}) \sim \gamma}[c(e, \tilde{e})].

Proposition 1.9 (Attainment). If $\mathcal{E}$ is compact and $c$ is continuous, then $\Gamma(\mu, \nu)$ is compact in the weak-* topology and the infimum in $W_c$ is attained.

Proof. By Prokhorov, the set of probability measures on the compact space $\mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$ is compact. The marginal constraints are closed, so $\Gamma(\mu, \nu)$ is compact. The map $\gamma \mapsto \int c \, d\gamma$ is continuous since $c$ is continuous and bounded on the compact $\mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$. A continuous function on a compact set attains its infimum. $\square$

Proposition 1.10 (Identity). If $\phi: \mathcal{E} \to \mathbb{R}^d$ is continuous and injective, then $W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \mu = \nu$.

Proof. $W_c(\mu, \nu) = W_2^2(\phi_\#\mu, \phi_\#\nu)$ by the pushforward identity. Wasserstein-2 vanishes iff the pushforwards coincide; injectivity of $\phi$ then forces $\mu = \nu$. $\square$

Lemma 1.11 (Triangle inequality on squared cost). For $c(e, \tilde{e}) = \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde{e})\|^2$,
c(e, \bar{e}) \le 2c(e, \tilde{e}) + 2c(\tilde{e}, \bar{e}).

Proof. Euclidean triangle inequality: $\|\phi(e) - \phi(\bar{e})\| \le \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde{e})\| + \|\phi(\tilde{e}) - \phi(\bar{e})\|$. Square and use $(a+b)^2 \le 2a^2 + 2b^2$. $\square$

1.4 Information-Theoretic Preliminaries

For probability measures $P, Q$ on a finite set $\mathcal{Y}$: $H(P) = -\sum_y P(y)\log P(y)$, $\mathrm{CE}(P,Q) = -\sum_y P(y) \log Q(y)$, $D_{KL}(P\|Q) = \sum_y P(y) \log(P(y)/Q(y))$.

Proposition 1.12 (Gibbs). $D_{KL}(P\|Q) \ge 0$ with equality iff $P = Q$. Consequently $\mathrm{CE}(P,Q) = H(P) + D_{KL}(P\|Q) \ge H(P)$.

1.5 Description Length (MDL) — Required for Rate Matching

Definition 1.13 (Description length). For a representation $\widetilde{M}$ in a class $\mathcal{C}$, define the description length $|R|(\widetilde{M})$ as the minimum number of bits required to encode $\widetilde{M}$ under a fixed universal prior over $\mathcal{C}$. For episodic memory $M = \{e_i\}_{i=1}^n$ with $e_i \in \mathcal{E}$, and for a labeled relational graph $G_M = (V_M, E_M)$ with labels $\mathcal{L}$:

|R|(M) = n \cdot \ell_e, \quad |R|(G_M) = |V_M| \log_2 |V_M| + |E_M| \log_2 |\mathcal{L}|,

where $\ell_e$ is the per-episode code length in bits. All rate comparisons in this work hold $|R|$ fixed across classes.

Definition 1.14 (Rate-distortion function). For a class $\mathcal{C}$ of representations and distortion functional $\mathrm{Err}: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$,
R_c^{\mathcal{C}}(D) = \inf\{|R|(\widetilde{M}) : \widetilde{M} \in \mathcal{C}, \, \mathrm{Err}(\widetilde{M}) \le D, \, P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde{M}) \text{ is } \mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}\text{-measurable}\}.

Proposition 1.15 (Monotonicity and class inclusion). $R_c^{\mathcal{C}}(D)$ is non-increasing in $D$. If $\mathcal{C}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{C}_2$, then $R_c^{\mathcal{C}_1}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathcal{C}_2}(D)$.

Proof. Feasible sets are nested increasing in $D$ and increasing under class inclusion. $\square$

Assumption 1.16 (Strict monotonicity of $R_c^{PMD}$). $R_c^{PMD}(D)$ is strictly decreasing on $[D_0, D_{\max}]$.

This is the assumption that closes Step 6 of the main theorem (see §4.4).

