3. Four Structural Lemmas

3.1 Spurious Liability (Lemma 1)

Assumption (A1). $S$ is predictive of $Y$ under $P_{\mathrm{tr}}$ but non-causal under $\mathcal{T}_{cf}$; $P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid S=s) \neq P(Y)$ on a set of $S^\star$-positive measure, where $S^\star$ is the intervention marginal of $S$ under $\mathcal{T}_{cf}$.

Lemma 3.1 (Spurious liability). Under (A1), the ML simulator on $M$ satisfies
U_f(M) = -\delta(M), \quad \delta(M) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} D_{KL}(P(Y) \| P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid s)) > 0.

Proof. $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} \mathbb{E}_{y \sim P(Y)}[-\log P_{\mathrm{tr}}(y \mid s)]$. Adding and subtracting $H(Y)$:
\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M) = H(Y) + \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} D_{KL}(P(Y) \| P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid s)) = H(Y) + \delta(M).


By Gibbs, each KL term is non-negative and strictly positive on a set of $S^\star$-positive measure by (A1), so $\delta(M) > 0$. Hence $U_f(M) = H(Y) - \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M) = -\delta(M) < 0$. $\square$

Corollary 3.2 (Initial excess cross-entropy). With $\epsilon(D) := \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(\mathcal{D}_D(M)) - H(Y)$ and $\mathcal{D}_0 = \mathrm{id}$,
\epsilon(0) = \delta(M).

This is the correction to the earlier claim $\epsilon(0) = 0$: the exact memory's excess cross-entropy is $\delta(M)$, not zero.

3.2 Invariance Criterion (Lemma 2)

Definition 3.3. $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(\widetilde{M}) = \sup_{Z \subseteq S} \sup_z \|P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde{M}) - P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid \widetilde{M})\|_{TV}$.

Lemma 3.4. $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(\widetilde{M}) = 0 \iff P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde{M})$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable.

Proof. Immediate from Theorem 1.7. $\square$

3.3 Non-Identification under Distribution-Preserving Retention (Lemma 3)

Definition 3.5. $M_{\mathrm{retain}} \subseteq M$ is distribution-preserving if $P_{M_{\mathrm{retain}}}(S, Y) = P_M(S, Y)$.

Lemma 3.6. Let $M_{\mathrm{retain}}$ be distribution-preserving and ML-estimated. Under FEC violation and (A1), $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(M_{\mathrm{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0$.

Proof. Distribution preservation gives $P_{M_{\mathrm{retain}}}(Y \mid S=s) = P_M(Y \mid S=s)$ for all $s$. By ML estimation and (A1),
P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \mid do(S=s), M_{\mathrm{retain}}) = P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid S=s) \neq P(Y).


FEC violation means no environment $g \in \mathcal{G}_{\mathrm{tr}}$ is excluded and none is introduced, so the same conditional persists. By Lemma 3.1's calculation, $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M_{\mathrm{retain}}) - H(Y) = \delta(M)$, hence $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(M_{\mathrm{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0$. $\square$

3.4 Geometric Separation (Lemma 4)

Definition 3.7. For a quotient-only abstraction $A$ with vertex map $A: V_M \to V_{A(M)}$, let $\phi(v)$ be the feature vector of the cluster $A^{-1}(v)$, and $\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)$ the closest $S$-invariant point. Let
\delta_0 = \inf_{v : \phi(v) \text{ not } S\text{-invariant}} \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\|.

Lemma 3.8 (Geometric separation). Under FEC violation, at any $D$ achieving $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurability:

(i) Any quotient-only $A$ that achieves measurability must collapse a vertex carrying both a spurious edge $(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc})$ and an invariant edge $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$.

(ii) The collapse forces a transport cost $\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}} \ge (\delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}})^2 > 0$ on the invariant structure, where $\epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}}$ is the maximum allowed causal-edge distortion, assumed $< \delta_0$.

(iii) Non-homomorphic distortion achieves measurability with zero such cost.

Proof.

Case 1 (preservation of spurious edge). If $A$ preserves $(A(e_S), A(e_Y), \mathrm{assoc}) \in E_{A(M)}$, then by Lemma 3.4 $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(A(M)) > 0$, so $A(M)$ is not $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable. Excluded.

Case 2 (collapse). The only way for a homomorphic $A$ to remove $(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc})$ is $A(e_S) = A(e_Y) =: v$. Measurability forces $\phi(v)$ to be $S$-invariant, so $\|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\| \ge \delta_0$. Preservation of $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$ forces $\|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\| \le \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}}$. By the triangle inequality,
\|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\| \ge \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\| - \|\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v) - \phi(e_S)\| \ge \delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}}.


Hence $c(e_S, v) \ge (\delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}})^2 > 0$, provided $\epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}} < \delta_0$. The transport cost is borne on the invariant edge $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$ through the displaced vertex $v$.

Case 3 (non-homomorphic). $\mathcal{D}_\theta$ rewrites $(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) \mapsto (e_S, \varnothing)$ without identifying $e_Y$ with $e_S$. The invariant edge $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$ is untouched, so no vertex displacement is required. $\square$

Remark 3.9 (Precise form of $o(\delta_0)$). The original Lemma 4 used $o(\delta_0)$ informally. The corrected version uses the explicit slack $\epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}} < \delta_0$, giving $\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}} \ge (\delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}})^2$. This is the transport cost up to the causal-edge tolerance.

Assumption (A6) (Rate–transport link). There exists a strictly increasing $\psi: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ with $\psi(0) = 0$ such that if a class $\mathcal{C}$ forces transport cost $\ge \Delta_{\mathrm{geo}}$ on the invariant structure, then
R_c^{\mathcal{C}}(D) \ge R_c^{PMD}(D) + \psi(\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}}) \quad \text{for all } D \ge D_0.

Corollary 3.10. Under (A6), for quotient-only $A$,
R_c^{A}(D) \ge R_c^{PMD}(D) + \Delta, \quad \Delta = \psi(\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}}) > 0.

