APPENDIX A — Measure-Theoretic and Probabilistic Foundations

A.1 Polish Spaces, Weak Convergence, Prokhorov

Definition A.1 (Polish space). A topological space is Polish if it is separable and completely metrizable. Every compact metric space is Polish.

Definition A.2 (Weak convergence). A sequence $(\mu_n)$ of probability measures on a Polish space $\mathcal{X}$ converges weakly to $\mu$, written $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$, if $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded continuous $f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$.

Definition A.3 (Tightness). A family $\mathcal{P}$ of probability measures on $\mathcal{X}$ is tight if for every $\epsilon > 0$ there is a compact $K \subseteq \mathcal{X}$ with $\mu(K) \ge 1 - \epsilon$ for all $\mu \in \mathcal{P}$.

Theorem A.4 (Prokhorov). A family $\mathcal{P}$ of probability measures on a Polish space is tight if and only if it is relatively compact in the weak topology.

Corollary A.5. If $\mathcal{X}$ is compact, every family of probability measures on $\mathcal{X}$ is tight, hence relatively compact.

Corollary A.6. If $\mathcal{X}$ is compact and $f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous, then $f$ is bounded and $\mu \mapsto \int f \, d\mu$ is continuous in the weak topology.

Proof. Continuous functions on compact spaces are bounded. Weak convergence is defined by convergence of integrals against bounded continuous functions. $\square$

A.2 Markov Kernels

Definition A.7. Let $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}})$ and $(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{Y}})$ be measurable spaces. A Markov kernel $K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$ is a map $K: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{Y}} \to [0,1]$ such that:

1. $x \mapsto K(x, B)$ is $\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}}$-measurable for each $B \in \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{Y}}$.
2. $B \mapsto K(x, B)$ is a probability measure for each $x \in \mathcal{X}$.

Definition A.8 (Composition). For $K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$ and $L: \mathcal{Y} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Z}$, the composition $LK: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Z}$ is $(LK)(x, C) = \int_{\mathcal{Y}} L(y, C) K(x, dy)$. Associativity holds: $(ML)K = M(LK)$.

Definition A.9 (Pushforward). For $K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$ and measurable $f: \mathcal{Y} \to \mathcal{Z}$, the pushforward $f_\# K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Z}$ is $(f_\# K)(x, C) = K(x, f^{-1}(C))$.

Definition A.10 (Kernel from a joint). For a probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$ with marginal $\mu_X$, the conditional kernel $K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$ satisfies $\mu(A \times B) = \int_A K(x, B) \mu_X(dx)$. It is $\mu_X$-a.e. unique (disintegration, Theorem A.14).

A.3 Total Variation

Definition A.11. For finite signed measures $\mu, \nu$ on $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}})$,
\|\mu - \nu\|_{TV} = \sup_{A \in \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{X}}} |\mu(A) - \nu(A)| = \tfrac{1}{2}\int |d\mu - d\nu|.


For kernels $K, L: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$,
\|K - L\|_{TV} = \sup_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \|K(x, \cdot) - L(x, \cdot)\|_{TV}.

Proposition A.12 (TV and bounded functions). For bounded measurable $f: \mathcal{Y} \to \mathbb{R}$ with $\|f\|_\infty \le 1$,
\left|\int f \, dK(x, \cdot) - \int f \, dL(x, \cdot)\right| \le 2\|K - L\|_{TV}.

Proof. Write $K - L = (K-L)^+ - (K-L)^-$ for the Jordan decomposition. Then $\int f \, d(K-L) = \int f \, d(K-L)^+ - \int f \, d(K-L)^-$, and $|\int f \, d(K-L)^\pm| \le \|f\|_\infty (K-L)^\pm(\mathcal{Y})$. Summing, $|\int f \, d(K-L)| \le (K-L)^+(\mathcal{Y}) + (K-L)^-(\mathcal{Y}) = \|K-L\|_1 = 2\|K-L\|_{TV}$. $\square$

Proposition A.13 (TV is a metric on kernels). The map $K \mapsto \|K\|_{TV}$ is a norm on the space of kernels, and $\|K - L\|_{TV} = 0$ iff $K = L$ everywhere.

Proof. Symmetry and triangle inequality are immediate from the definition. For the identity: if $\|K - L\|_{TV} = 0$, then for every $x$ and every measurable $B$, $|K(x,B) - L(x,B)| = 0$, so $K(x, \cdot) = L(x, \cdot)$ for every $x$. $\square$

A.4 Disintegration

Theorem A.14 (Disintegration). Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$ with marginal $\mu_X$ on $\mathcal{X}$. Then there exists a $\mu_X$-a.e. unique Markov kernel $K: \mathcal{X} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{Y}$ with $\mu(A \times B) = \int_A K(x, B) \mu_X(dx)$ for all measurable $A \subseteq \mathcal{X}, B \subseteq \mathcal{Y}$.

Corollary A.15 (Bayes). Under the setup of Theorem A.14, the conditional $P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid S = s)$ is well-defined $P_{\mathrm{tr}}(S)$-a.e.

