APPENDIX B — Structural Causal Models and Counterfactual Invariance

B.1 Structural Causal Models

Definition B.1. A structural causal model is a tuple $W = (U, V, F)$ with:

· $U = (U_1, \dots, U_k)$ exogenous, jointly independent.
· $V = (V_1, \dots, V_k)$ endogenous.
· Structural equations $V_i = f_i(\mathrm{pa}(V_i), U_i)$ with $\mathrm{pa}(V_i) \subseteq V \cup U \setminus \{V_i\}$.

The causal graph $G(W)$ has vertices $V \cup U$ and directed edges from each parent to its child.

Assumption B.2 (Acyclicity). $G(W)$ is acyclic. Then $(V_1, \dots, V_k)$ has a unique solution as a function of $U$.

Definition B.3 (Observational distribution). $P_W$ is the distribution of $(V, U)$ induced by $P_U$ and $F$.

In this work, $W = (C, S, Y)$ with $C = f_C(U_C)$, $S = f_S(C, U_S)$, $Y = f_Y(C, S, U_Y)$.

B.2 Interventions

Definition B.4. For $Z \subseteq V$ and value $z$, the intervention $do(Z=z)$ replaces the structural equations for $Z$ with constants $z$. The resulting SCM is $W_{do(Z=z)}$, with interventional distribution $P(\cdot \mid do(Z=z)) = P_{W_{do(Z=z)}}$.

Definition B.5 (Intervention protocol). An intervention protocol $\mathcal{T}_{cf}$ is a probability distribution over interventions $do(X=x)$.

Remark B.6 (Modularity). $do(Z=z)$ modifies only the equations for $Z$. All other equations remain unchanged.

B.3 Counterfactuals

Definition B.7. For $do(Z=z)$, the counterfactual $V_i(z)$ is its value in $W_{do(Z=z)}$ under the same exogenous $U$: $V_i(z) = f_i(\mathrm{pa}(V_i)(z), U_i)$.

Definition B.8 (Twin network). The twin network contains both factual $V$ and counterfactual $V(z)$, sharing $U$.

B.4 The Counterfactual-Invariant $\sigma$-Algebra

Definition B.9. For $Z \subseteq S$ and $\{do(Z=z)\}_z$,
\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp} = \sigma\bigl(\{X(z)\}_z\bigr) \cap \sigma(X).

Interpretation B.10. An event $A \in \mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$ has indicator $\mathbf{1}_A(X)$ satisfying $\mathbf{1}_A(X(z)) = \mathbf{1}_A(X)$ for all $z$. So $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$ captures exactly the information robust to interventions on $Z$.

B.5 Veitch's Invariance Criterion

Theorem B.11. A kernel $P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde{M})$ satisfies
P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde{M}) = P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \mid \widetilde{M}) \quad \forall Z \subseteq S, \forall z


if and only if $P_{\mathrm{sim}}$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable.

Proof.

($\Leftarrow$) Sufficiency. Suppose $P_{\mathrm{sim}}$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable. Then for each $B$, $P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid do(X))$ is a $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable function of $X$. By definition of $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$, such a function is invariant under $X \mapsto X(z)$ for all $z$, hence
P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde{M}) = P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid \widetilde{M}).

($\Rightarrow$) Necessity. Suppose invariance holds for all $Z \subseteq S$ and all $z$. Then for each $B$, $X \mapsto P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid do(X))$ is invariant under all interventions on $Z$, hence is in $\sigma(\{X(z)\}_z) \cap \sigma(X) = \mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$. Since this holds for all $B$, $P_{\mathrm{sim}}$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable. $\square$

Remark B.12. It suffices to check $Z = S$ (maximal), since invariance under $do(S=s)$ for all $s$ implies invariance under any $Z \subseteq S$.

