APPENDIX C — Optimal Transport and Wasserstein Geometry

C.1 Couplings

Definition C.1. Let $\mu, \nu$ be probability measures on a Polish space $(\mathcal{E}, d)$. A coupling is a probability measure $\gamma$ on $\mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$ with marginals $\mu, \nu$. Write $\Gamma(\mu, \nu)$ for the set of all couplings.

Proposition C.2 (Non-emptiness). $\Gamma(\mu, \nu) \neq \emptyset$; the product measure $\mu \otimes \nu$ is always a coupling.

Proposition C.3 (Convexity). $\Gamma(\mu, \nu)$ is convex.

C.2 Kantorovich Duality

Theorem C.4 (Kantorovich). Let $\mu, \nu$ be probability measures on a Polish space $\mathcal{E}$ and $c: \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E} \to [0, \infty)$ lower semicontinuous. Then
\inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int c \, d\gamma = \sup_{(\phi, \psi) \in \Phi_c}\left\{\int \phi \, d\mu + \int \psi \, d\nu\right\},


where $\Phi_c = \{(\phi, \psi) : \phi(e) + \psi(\tilde{e}) \le c(e, \tilde{e}) \text{ bounded continuous}\}$.

C.3 Existence of Optimal Coupling

Corollary C.5. If $\mathcal{E}$ is compact and $c$ continuous, the infimum is attained.

Proof. By Corollary A.5, the space of probability measures on the compact $\mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$ is compact. The set $\Gamma(\mu, \nu)$ is closed (marginal constraints are preserved under weak limits), hence compact. The functional $\gamma \mapsto \int c \, d\gamma$ is continuous since $c$ is bounded continuous on the compact space. Attainment follows from Weierstrass. $\square$

C.4 Wasserstein Distance

Definition C.6. For $p \ge 1$, $W_p(\mu, \nu) = \left(\inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int d(e, \tilde{e})^p \, d\gamma\right)^{1/p}$.

Proposition C.7 (Metric properties). $W_p$ is a metric.

Proof. Symmetry is from $d(e, \tilde{e}) = d(\tilde{e}, e)$. Triangle inequality is the gluing lemma (Villani, Ch. 6). Identity: if $W_p(\mu, \nu) = 0$, the optimal coupling is supported on the diagonal, forcing $\mu = \nu$. $\square$

C.5 Feature-Based Wasserstein

Definition C.8. For $\phi: \mathcal{E} \to \mathbb{R}^d$, $c(e, \tilde{e}) = \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde{e})\|^2$,
W_c(\mu, \nu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde{e})\|^2 \, d\gamma = W_2^2(\phi_\#\mu, \phi_\#\nu).

Proposition C.9 (Pushforward identity). $W_c(\mu, \nu) = W_2^2(\phi_\#\mu, \phi_\#\nu)$.

Proof. For $\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)$, $(\phi \times \phi)_\#\gamma \in \Gamma(\phi_\#\mu, \phi_\#\nu)$, and the integral rewrites as $\int \|u - v\|^2 \, d(\phi \times \phi)_\#\gamma$. Conversely, every coupling of pushforwards lifts to a coupling of $\mu, \nu$ by the measurable selection theorem. Infima coincide. $\square$

Proposition C.10 (Injectivity). If $\phi$ is injective, $W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \mu = \nu$. If not injective, $W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \phi_\#\mu = \phi_\#\nu$.

Proof. By C.9 and C.7, $W_c = 0 \iff \phi_\#\mu = \phi_\#\nu$. If $\phi$ injective, $\phi^{-1}$ well-defined on $\phi(\mathcal{E})$, so $\mu = \nu$. $\square$

C.6 Triangle Inequality

Lemma C.11. For all $e, \tilde{e}, \bar{e} \in \mathcal{E}$, $\|\phi(e) - \phi(\bar{e})\| \le \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde{e})\| + \|\phi(\tilde{e}) - \phi(\bar{e})\|$.

Corollary C.12. $c(e, \bar{e}) \le 2c(e, \tilde{e}) + 2c(\tilde{e}, \bar{e})$.

Proof. Square the triangle inequality and use $(a+b)^2 \le 2a^2 + 2b^2$. $\square$

