APPENDIX D — Information-Theoretic Foundations

D.1 Entropy, Cross-Entropy, KL

Definition D.1. For distributions $P, Q$ on a finite set $\mathcal{Y}$:
H(P) = -\sum_y P(y)\log P(y), \quad \mathrm{CE}(P,Q) = -\sum_y P(y)\log Q(y), \quad D_{KL}(P\|Q) = \sum_y P(y)\log\frac{P(y)}{Q(y)},


with conventions $0\log 0 = 0$ and $\mathrm{CE} = +\infty$ if $P(y) > 0$ and $Q(y) = 0$.

Proposition D.2. $\mathrm{CE}(P,Q) = H(P) + D_{KL}(P\|Q)$.

Proof. $-\sum_y P(y)\log Q(y) = -\sum_y P(y)\log P(y) + \sum_y P(y)\log(P(y)/Q(y))$. $\square$

D.2 Gibbs' Inequality

Theorem D.3 (Gibbs). $D_{KL}(P\|Q) \ge 0$ with equality iff $P = Q$.

Proof. Since $-\log$ is convex, Jensen gives
D_{KL}(P\|Q) = \sum_y P(y)\left(-\log\frac{Q(y)}{P(y)}\right) \ge -\log\sum_y P(y)\frac{Q(y)}{P(y)} = -\log 1 = 0.


Equality in Jensen requires $Q(y)/P(y)$ constant $P$-a.e., i.e., $P = Q$. $\square$

Corollary D.4. $\mathrm{CE}(P,Q) \ge H(P)$, with equality iff $P = Q$.

D.3 Continuity of Cross-Entropy under TV

Proposition D.5. Let $P$ be a distribution on finite $\mathcal{Y}$ with $P(y) > 0$ for all $y$. Let $Q, Q'$ satisfy $Q'(y) \ge \alpha > 0$ for all $y$. Then
|\mathrm{CE}(P,Q) - \mathrm{CE}(P,Q')| \le \frac{2}{\alpha}\|Q - Q'\|_{TV}.

Proof. For each $y$ with $Q'(y) \ge \alpha$, mean value theorem gives
|\log Q(y) - \log Q'(y)| \le \frac{|Q(y) - Q'(y)|}{\min(Q(y), Q'(y))} \le \frac{|Q(y) - Q'(y)|}{\alpha}.


Multiply by $P(y) \le 1$, sum over $y$, use $\|\cdot\|_1 \le 2\|\cdot\|_{TV}$. $\square$

Corollary D.6. If $Q_n \to Q$ in TV and $Q$ bounded below by $\alpha > 0$, then $\mathrm{CE}(P, Q_n) \to \mathrm{CE}(P, Q)$.

Corollary D.7 (Kernel version). If $K_n \to K$ in kernel-TV and $K(x, \cdot)$ bounded below by $\alpha > 0$ for all $x$, then $\int \mathrm{CE}(P, K_n(x,\cdot)) \, \mu(dx) \to \int \mathrm{CE}(P, K(x,\cdot)) \, \mu(dx)$ for any probability $\mu$.

Proof. The bound of Proposition D.5 holds uniformly in $x$ by the kernel-TV definition; integrate against $\mu$. $\square$

D.4 Data Processing Inequality

Theorem D.8. If $X \to Y \to Z$ is a Markov chain, then $I(X; Z) \le I(X; Y)$.

Proof. Standard (Cover & Thomas, Ch. 2). $\square$

