APPENDIX E — Full Proofs of the Four Structural Lemmas

E.1 Lemma 1 — Spurious Liability

Assumption (A1). $S$ is predictive of $Y$ under $P_{\mathrm{tr}}$ but non-causal under $\mathcal{T}_{cf}$; $P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid S=s) \neq P(Y)$ on a set of $S^\star$-positive measure, where $S^\star$ is the intervention marginal of $S$ under $\mathcal{T}_{cf}$.

Lemma E.1. Under (A1), the ML simulator on $M$ satisfies
U_f(M) = -\delta(M), \quad \delta(M) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} D_{KL}(P(Y) \| P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid s)) > 0.

Proof. With $X = S$ and $\mathcal{T}_{cf}$ randomizing $S \sim S^\star$,
\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} \mathbb{E}_{y \sim P(Y)}[-\log P_{\mathrm{tr}}(y \mid s)].


Add and subtract $H(Y) = \mathbb{E}_{y \sim P(Y)}[-\log P(Y)]$:
\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M) - H(Y) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} \mathbb{E}_{y \sim P(Y)}\left[\log\frac{P(Y)}{P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid s)}\right] = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim S^\star} D_{KL}(P(Y) \| P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid s)).


By Gibbs, each term is non-negative and strictly positive on a set of $S^\star$-positive measure by (A1). So $\delta(M) > 0$ and $U_f(M) = H(Y) - \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M) = -\delta(M)$. $\square$

Corollary E.2. With $\epsilon(D) = \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(\mathcal{D}_D(M)) - H(Y)$ and $\mathcal{D}_0 = \mathrm{id}$, $\epsilon(0) = \delta(M)$.

Remark E.3. The cross-entropy formulation is essential. If one instead used $\mathbb{E}_{s}[H(P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid s))]$, the inequality would fail when $S^\star = P_{\mathrm{tr}}(S)$ since conditioning reduces entropy on average. Cross-entropy avoids this by measuring the coding cost of the interventional marginal under the MLE's conditionals.

E.2 Lemma 2 — Invariance Criterion

Definition E.4. $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(\widetilde{M}) = \sup_{Z \subseteq S} \sup_z \|P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde{M}) - P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid \widetilde{M})\|_{TV}$.

Lemma E.5. $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(\widetilde{M}) = 0 \iff P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde{M})$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable.

Proof. ($\Rightarrow$) If $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}} = 0$, then for all $Z \subseteq S$, $z$, and every measurable $B$:
P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde{M}) = P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid \widetilde{M}).


So the map $X \mapsto P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \in B \mid do(X))$ is invariant under all $do(Z=z)$. By Theorem B.11, the kernel is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable.

($\Leftarrow$) If $P_{\mathrm{sim}}$ is $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable, it does not depend on $Z \subseteq S$ by definition of $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$, so the sup defining $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}$ is zero. $\square$

E.3 Lemma 3 — Non-Identification under Distribution-Preserving Retention

Definition E.6. $M_{\mathrm{retain}} \subseteq M$ is distribution-preserving if $P_{M_{\mathrm{retain}}}(S,Y) = P_M(S,Y)$.

Lemma E.7. Let $M_{\mathrm{retain}}$ be distribution-preserving, ML-estimated. Under FEC violation and (A1),
\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(M_{\mathrm{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0.

Proof. Distribution preservation gives $P_{M_{\mathrm{retain}}}(Y \mid S=s) = P_M(Y \mid S=s)$ for all $s$. By ML estimation,
P_{\mathrm{sim}}(\hat{Y} \mid do(S=s), M_{\mathrm{retain}}) = P_{M_{\mathrm{retain}}}(Y \mid S=s) = P_M(Y \mid S=s) = P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid S=s).


FEC violation: there exists $h$ such that $P(Y \mid h(S), g) = P(Y \mid h(S), g')$ for all $g, g' \in \mathcal{G}_{\mathrm{tr}}$ and the common value differs from $P(Y)$. Since $M_{\mathrm{retain}}$ preserves $(S,Y)$ distribution, no environment is excluded or introduced. The ML simulator recovers $P_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y \mid S=s) \neq P(Y)$. By Lemma E.1's calculation, $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{sim}}(M_{\mathrm{retain}}) - H(Y) = \delta(M) > 0$, so $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(M_{\mathrm{retain}}) = \delta(M)$. $\square$

Remark E.8 (Degenerate subsets). If $M_{\mathrm{retain}} = \{e : S(e) = s_0\}$, then $S$ is constant, ML cannot condition, and $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}} = 0$ trivially. But this violates distribution preservation. At matched rate $R$ (A3), such a subset wastes bits and is dominated.

Remark E.9. No subset of $M$ can contain falsifying evidence that $M$ lacked. Deletion removes data; it cannot create the environmental diversity whose absence constitutes FEC violation.

E.4 Lemma 4 — Geometric Separation (Corrected)

Definition E.10. For quotient-only $A$ with vertex map $A: V_M \to V_{A(M)}$, let $\phi(v)$ be the feature vector of cluster $A^{-1}(v)$, and $\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)$ the closest $S$-invariant point. Define
\delta_0 = \inf_{v : \phi(v) \text{ not } S\text{-invariant}} \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\|.

Lemma E.11 (Geometric separation). Under FEC violation, at any $D$ achieving $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurability:

(i) Any quotient-only $A$ achieving measurability must collapse a vertex carrying both a spurious edge $(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc})$ and an invariant edge $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$.

(ii) This collapse forces a transport cost $\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}} \ge (\delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}})^2 > 0$ on the invariant structure, where $\epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}} < \delta_0$ is the maximum allowed causal-edge distortion.

(iii) Non-homomorphic $\mathcal{D}_\theta$ achieves measurability with zero such cost.

Proof.

Case 1 (preservation). If $(A(e_S), A(e_Y), \mathrm{assoc}) \in E_{A(M)}$, then $A(M)$ retains the spurious relation. By Lemma E.5, $\mathrm{Err}_{\mathrm{spur}}(A(M)) > 0$, so $A(M)$ is not $\mathcal{F}_{X_Z^\perp}$-measurable. Excluded.

Case 2 (collapse). The only homomorphic way for $A$ to remove $(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc})$ is $A(e_S) = A(e_Y) =: v$. Measurability requires $\phi(v)$ to be $S$-invariant, so $\|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\| \ge \delta_0$. Preservation of $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$ through $v$ forces $\|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\| \le \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}}$. Now:
\|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\| \ge \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\| - \|\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v) - \phi(e_S)\| \ge \delta_0 - 0 = \delta_0.


Wait — more carefully: $\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)$ is the closest $S$-invariant point to $\phi(v)$. Since $\phi(e_S)$ lies in the non-invariant region and $\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)$ is invariant, $\|\phi(e_S) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\| \ge 0$ trivially, but the relevant bound is
\|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\| \ge \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)\| - \|\phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v) - \phi(e_S)\| \ge \delta_0 - \delta_0 = 0?


This is too weak. The correct argument uses the fact that $\phi(e_Y)$ is (near) $S$-invariant. We have $\|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\| \le \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}}$, and $\phi(e_Y) \approx \phi_{\mathrm{inv}}(v)$ since $e_Y$ is the target vertex (invariant). Then:
\|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\| \ge \|\phi(e_S) - \phi(e_Y)\| - \|\phi(e_Y) - \phi(v)\| \ge \delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}}.


Hence $c(e_S, v) \ge (\delta_0 - \epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}})^2 > 0$ when $\epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}} < \delta_0$. The transport cost is borne on the invariant edge $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$ through the displaced vertex $v$.

Case 3 (non-homomorphic). $\mathcal{D}_\theta$ rewrites $(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) \mapsto (e_S, \varnothing)$ without identifying $e_Y$ with $e_S$. The invariant edge $(e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal})$ is untouched, so no vertex displacement is required. $\square$

Remark E.12. The original version used $o(\delta_0)$ informally; the corrected version uses the explicit slack $\epsilon_{\mathrm{causal}} < \delta_0$.

Assumption E.13 (Rate–transport link, A6). There exists strictly increasing $\psi: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ with $\psi(0) = 0$ such that if class $\mathcal{C}$ forces transport cost $\ge \Delta_{\mathrm{geo}}$ on the invariant structure, then
R_c^{\mathcal{C}}(D) \ge R_c^{PMD}(D) + \psi(\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}}) \quad \text{for all } D \ge D_0.

Corollary E.14. Under (A6), for quotient-only $A$,
R_c^A(D) \ge R_c^{PMD}(D) + \Delta, \quad \Delta = \psi(\Delta_{\mathrm{geo}}) > 0.

